WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.430
At seven in the morning, a

2
00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:03.250
parent called me.

3
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:05.900
“Why is the school page

4
00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.100
selling cheap laptops?”

5
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:12.340
Our page had been taken over in the night,

6
00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:15.480
and nobody knew who should do what.

7
00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:18.990
Today: how to run a thirty-minute

8
00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:21.250
incident drill, so your school knows

9
00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:22.930
what to do before it happens.

10
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.880
A tabletop drill is a story your

11
00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.200
staff work through around a table,

12
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:30.980
with no computers needed.

13
00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:33.070
You tell it in three short

14
00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:34.950
updates, one after another.

15
00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:38.210
After each one, the team says what they

16
00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:40.270
would do, and someone writes it down.

17
00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.080
Give out four roles.

18
00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:45.250
The incident lead decides.

19
00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.100
The communications lead is the

20
00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:49.540
only voice to parents.

21
00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:51.700
The technical helper deals

22
00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.440
with accounts and devices.

23
00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:55.830
And the record keeper writes down

24
00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:57.390
what happened, and when.

25
00:00:58.590 --> 00:00:59.890
Pick a story that could

26
00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:01.210
really happen to you.

27
00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:03.540
The school page taken over,

28
00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:05.600
posting fake fee offers.

29
00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:08.030
Ransomware on the office

30
00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:10.390
computer two days before exams.

31
00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:12.990
Or a teacher’s WhatsApp taken

32
00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:14.710
over, asking parents for money.

33
00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:17.120
It’s like a fire drill.

34
00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:20.070
Nobody waits for a real fire to

35
00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:21.650
find out the exit is blocked.

36
00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:25.490
A cyber drill finds the blocked exits: the

37
00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:28.010
missing password, the backup nobody can

38
00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:30.770
find, the phone number nobody has.

39
00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:32.560
Quick check!

40
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.300
In our drill, the page is still

41
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:37.580
posting scam links, and parents

42
00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:39.020
are calling every teacher.

43
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.120
Who should answer them?

44
00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:44.770
The communications lead, with one short

45
00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:46.970
message on a channel parents already

46
00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:47.610
trust.

47
00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:49.660
“Our page was taken over.

48
00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.130
Don’t pay anything it asks for.

49
00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:54.110
We’ll update you by five.”

50
00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:55.650
Everyone else points

51
00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:56.770
parents to that message.

52
00:01:58.140 --> 00:01:59.720
Drills test the boring

53
00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:01.280
things that save you.

54
00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:04.830
Can you find the page’s recovery email?

55
00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.320
Where is last week’s backup?

56
00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:10.870
And if the internet is down, is there a

57
00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:12.690
paper card with the key phone numbers?

58
00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:15.690
When the drill ends, write

59
00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:16.850
down three lessons.

60
00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:20.050
Change one thing for each: the

61
00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:22.750
contact card, a second admin for the

62
00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:24.470
page, where the backup lives.

63
00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:26.600
Then finish with a quick

64
00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.120
spot-the-scam round, with everyone’s

65
00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:31.000
agreement and no names on the scores.

66
00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:33.120
Your turn.

67
00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:35.980
In our story, a teacher

68
00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:37.880
clicked a fake link first.

69
00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:40.370
Should the drill end by

70
00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:42.470
finding someone to blame?

71
00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:46.410
No: people who fear blame hide

72
00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.870
mistakes, and hidden mistakes grow.

73
00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:52.190
Remember: a real story in three

74
00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:54.990
updates, four roles, one voice to

75
00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:57.330
parents, and real changes afterwards.

76
00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.300
Try it at school tomorrow: ask your

77
00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:03.080
head teacher for thirty minutes of

78
00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:04.140
the next staff meeting.

79
00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:06.810
Bring one scenario on paper,

80
00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:08.610
and four role cards.
